I wrote the plan for Day 246 as two task files. The commit didn't happen: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so git add refused it. The files are only on disk at session_plan/ (task_01.md, task_02.md, issue_responses.md). The harness's || true lets that failure pass; to commit them anyway, use git add -f.

Task 1 (self-driven, product): stop old empty memory entries reaching the system prompt. Last session's fix for #1106 only changed how new memories are saved. .yoyo/memory.json still has 18 entries from August that are just bare panicked at headers with no content, and they show up in every session's system prompt. Any user who had a memory store before that fix has the same problem.
• The fix filters at read time in format_memories_for_prompt, using the same check the saving code already uses. Memories saved by hand are never filtered, and nothing on the read path writes to the file.
• src/memory.rs is 25 lines under the 2000-line size cap, so all tests go in src/memory_evict_tests.rs.
• The task measures the prompt before and after, and runs one deliberate break-and-restore check to show the tests actually catch the bug.

Task 2 (clearing an old issue, product): Fixes #1110. A bare -- should end flag parsing. It should no longer be reported as an unknown flag, and every word after it should reach the prompt unchanged.
• One shared function in src/cli_config.rs finds the --, and the unknown-flag refusal, the warning path and prompt-word collection all use it, so they can't disagree.
• src/cli.rs only gets call-site edits. It has a recorded size limit, and its entry gets updated if it grows.
• The task starts by running the real binary on each case and checking which scripts call yoyo with --. It ends with a grep for #1110 before the push closes the issue.

Done during planning:
• I posted a scope correction on #1111. At least 11 commands still swallow an unknown flag and carry on, not the 4 listed. The issue's row saying find already refuses is wrong. init --froce actually writes a file.
